UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 19, Lesson 1: Solving disagreements', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Disagreements are normal when people have different ideas. A fair way to solve one is: stop and calm down, say what happened using an ''I'' statement, listen to the other person, think of options together and choose one that is fair to everyone. If the problem is too big, or someone is unsafe or being hurt, ask a trusted adult for help. Hurting people or things never solves a disagreement.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nTwo friends disagree about who owns a drawing they made together. Write the first two steps of a fair solution, using an ''I'' statement.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStep 1: stop and breathe. Step 2: ''I feel worried because I put a lot of work into the drawing and I would like us to decide together.'' This begins calmly and shares the problem without blame.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it. A good solution is one that both people can accept.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a five-step disagreement plan as a flowchart and use it on two made-up problems (a game rule and a shared item). For each, write a fair solution and name when an adult must help.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Disagreements are normal when people have different ideas. A fair way to solve one is: stop and calm down, say what happened using an ''I'' statement, listen to the other person, think of options together and choose one that is fair to everyone. If the problem is too big, or someone is unsafe or being hurt, ask a trusted adult for help. Hurting people or things never solves a disagreement.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a five-step disagreement plan as a flowchart and use it on two made-up problems (a game rule and a shared item). For each, write a fair solution and name when an adult must help. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a disagreement on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it.. Correction: Step 1: stop and breathe.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it. A good solution is one that both people can accept. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a disagreement on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a disagreement, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a disagreement with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a disagreement. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a disagreement. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Step 1: stop and breathe"],"question":"Two friends disagree about who owns a drawing they made together. Write the first two steps of a fair solution, using an ''I'' statement. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a disagreement; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of calming down and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it","Disagreements are normal when people have different ideas","Step 1: stop and breathe"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a disagreement?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named calming down and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it","Name calming down and explain why it matters only, without linking it to a disagreement","Apply a disagreement to new material and name the calming down and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies a disagreement with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on a disagreement. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Step 1: stop and breathe","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for a disagreement?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with a disagreement","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a disagreement?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Disagreements are normal when people have different ideas.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example: two children both want the last turn on the swing","Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a disagreement?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for a disagreement?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Write a five-step disagreement plan as a flowchart and use it on two made-up problems (a game rule and a shared item)","Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it","Name calming down and explain why it matters only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies a disagreement to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a disagreement with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a disagreement on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a disagreement on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a disagreement. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a disagreement, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it.. Correction: Step 1: stop and breathe.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w19-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a disagreement.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use a disagreement with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify calming down and explain why it matters.","I can practise an I statement independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a fair solution needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disagreements are normal when people have different ideas.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Disagreements are normal when people have different ideas","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a disagreement with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: two children both want the last turn on the swing. Step 1: calm down with a slow breath. Step 2: ''I feel upset because I wanted a turn.'' Step 3: listen (''I only had one go''). Step 4: options (take turns, 10 swings each, ask a teacher). Step 5: choose 10 swings each, then swap. They say thank you.\n\nTRY FIRST\nTwo friends disagree about who owns a drawing they made together. Write the first two steps of a fair solution, using an ''I'' statement.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStep 1: stop and breathe. Step 2: ''I feel worried because I put a lot of work into the drawing and I would like us to decide together.'' This begins calmly and shares the problem without blame.","explain":"Disagreements are normal when people have different ideas. A fair way to solve one is: stop and calm down, say what happened using an ''I'' statement, listen to the other person, think of options together and choose one that is fair to everyone. If the problem is too big, or someone is unsafe or being hurt, ask a trusted adult for help. Hurting people or things never solves a disagreement.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it. A good solution is one that both people can accept. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"First steps in solving a disagreement.","caption":"Calm first, then speak.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Calm</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">stop and breathe</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Say it</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">I feel upset because</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: two children both want the last turn on the swing. Step 1: calm down with a slow breath. Step 2: ''I feel upset  Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: two children both want the last turn on the swing. Step 1: calm down with a slow breath. Step 2: ''I feel upset  Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a disagreement with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: two children both want the last turn on the swing. Step 1: calm down with a slow breath. Step 2: ''I feel upset because I wanted a turn.'' Step 3: listen (''I only had one go''). Step 4: options (take turns, 10 swings each, ask a teacher). Step 5: choose 10 swings each, then swap. They say thank you. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Options and when to ask an adult.","caption":"Pick a fair option.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Options</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">take turns, 10 swings each</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Adult help</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">if someone is unsafe</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a disagreement with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it. A good solution is one that both people can accept. Here is a more helpful example: Example: two children both want the last turn on the swing. Step 1: calm down with a slow breath. Step 2: ''I feel upset because I wanted a turn.'' Step 3: listen (''I only had one go''). Step 4: options (take turns, 10 swings each, ask a teacher). Step 5: choose 10 swings each, then swap. They say thank you.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStep 1: stop and breathe. Step 2: ''I feel worried because I put a lot of work into the drawing and I would like us to decide together.'' This begins calmly and shares the problem without blame.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking that the loudest person, or the winner of an argument, has solved it. A good solution is one that both people can accept. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a disagreement with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDisagreements are normal when people have different ideas. A fair way to solve one is: stop and calm down, say what happened using an ''I'' statement, listen to the other person, think of options together and choose one that is fair to everyone. If the problem is too big, or someone is unsafe or being hurt, ask a trusted adult for help. Hurting people or things never solves a disagreement. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 19, Lesson 1: Solving disagreements","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Friendships and respect","week":19,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: two children both want the last turn on the swing. Step 1: calm down with a slow breath. Step 2: ''I feel upset because I wanted a turn.'' Step 3: listen (''I only had one go''). Step 4: options (take turns, 10 swings each, ask a teacher). Step 5: choose 10 swings each, then swap. They say thank you.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"First steps in solving a disagreement.","caption":"Calm first, then speak.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Calm</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">stop and breathe</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Say it</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">I feel upset because</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Options and when to ask an adult.","caption":"Pick a fair option.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Options</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">take turns, 10 swings each</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Adult help</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">if someone is unsafe</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w19-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
